#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186143632} :activity/id #uuid "78db3c1b-5958-5d06-912e-feba2d0d67f3" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050599 :topic/id #uuid "576878c2-12a4-51b4-9889-1011d89192c4" :topic/math-academy-id 2360} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186143632 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186144029 :tutorial/content "We can use models to multiply fractions by whole numbers. But can we do this without using models?\n\nAs it turns out, models give us vital clues on how this should be done!\n\nTo demonstrate, let's take a look at the fraction model for $\\dfrac 2 9 \\times 3\\mathbin{:}$\n\n![](images/47/4700c7ddbdc849a73d69ba41d2d716218f1fb181e8e5e9b8757392db6d9a6243.png)\n\nIn the answer, we end up with ${\\color{blue}{2}}\\times {\\color{red}{3}} = 6$ parts, and the denominator remains the same. This is always the case. \n\nTherefore, to multiply a fraction by a whole number, we follow the following steps:\n\n**Step 1**: We multiply the numerator by the whole number.\n\n**Step 2**: We keep the denominator the same.\n\nUsing these steps, we can figure out the answer without using models:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\color{blue}2}{9}{\\color{red}\\,\\times 3\\,} = \\dfrac{{\\color{blue}{2}}{\\color{red}\\,\\times 3\\,}}{9} =  \\dfrac{6}{9}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "e1ad38dc-1875-52f8-ad8b-fb013451e541" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8393 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "ec9e5f6f-5761-5bfe-a807-c02ebc68c07b" :step/math-academy-id 130656 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186143633}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186143633 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186142772 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186142510 :question/id #uuid "714ed22d-8268-502d-9d74-12620cb1c4fe" :question/math-academy-id "e-13393"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9c58126c-3d5b-5c2e-aaa4-7f9304e77935" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050244}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 1759